<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<section xml:id="sec-6-9-javadoc">
  <title>Documenting Classes with Javadoc</title>

  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      In Chapter 4, you learned how to document individual static methods using Javadoc comments, 
      detailing preconditions, postconditions, <c>@param</c> tags, and <c>@return</c> tags. 
      When documenting an entire object-oriented class, our documentation responsibilities expand. 
      A class is not just a collection of disconnected functions; it is a blueprint for stateful 
      objects that live on the heap.
    </p>

    <p>
      Documenting a class introduces three essential concepts:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          The Class-Level Javadoc Comment (describing the high-level abstraction and its class invariants).
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Preserving Information Hiding in Documentation (why public documentation must never reveal 
          private fields or internal data structures).
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Documenting Constructors and Method Contracts Against State (why constructor Javadoc omits 
          <c>@return</c>, and how method contracts interact with internal fields).
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </introduction>



  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-9-1-class-level-comment">
    <title>The Class-Level Comment and Class Invariants</title>

    <p>
      A class-level Javadoc comment is placed immediately above the <c>public class ...</c> 
      declaration. Its purpose is to explain to other programmers what real-world concept or 
      mathematical abstraction this class models, without describing how it is implemented inside.
    </p>

    <p>
      Most importantly, the class-level comment is where you specify the class's <em>class invariants</em>. 
      A class invariant is an assertion about an object's state that is guaranteed to be true 
      throughout the lifetime of every valid object of that class. It must be established by every 
      constructor and preserved by every public mutator method.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, consider our <c>Rectangle</c> and <c>ElectricVehicle</c> classes:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          In a <c>Rectangle</c> class, an invariant might be: The width and height are strictly 
          positive finite numbers, and the top-left corner reference is never <c>null</c>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          In an <c>ElectricVehicle</c> class, an invariant might be: The current battery charge 
          is always between <c>0.0</c> and the maximum battery capacity, the efficiency rating 
          is strictly positive, and the total odometer mileage is non-negative and never decreases 
          (a car cannot travel negative miles).
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Notice that not every class requires strict invariants. In our simple <c>Point</c> class, 
      the coordinates <c>x</c> and <c>y</c> represent arbitrary Cartesian positions on an infinite 
      plane; because any finite real number<mdash/>positive, negative, or zero<mdash/>is a completely 
      legal coordinate, <c>Point</c> has no domain-specific invariants beyond holding valid 
      <c>double</c> values. Most real-world classes, however, model physical objects or engineering 
      systems where specific boundaries must always hold true.
    </p>

    <p>
      When clients read your documentation, the class invariants tell them what baseline assumptions 
      they can safely rely on at all times.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-9-2-preserving-information-hiding">
    <title>Preserving Information Hiding in Documentation</title>

    <p>
      When documenting a class, it is natural to want to summarize the code you just wrote. 
      For example, you might be tempted to write a comment like this:
    </p>

    <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
/**
 * Represents a 2D geometric point. Stores the coordinates in two
 * private double variables named x and y on the heap.
 */
public class Point { ... }
    </program>

    <p>
      While this comment accurately describes the lines of code inside the file, it works against 
      two key principles of good documentation:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>It repeats the code:</strong> Anyone reading the source code can already see 
          the declarations <c>private double x;</c> and <c>private double y;</c>. Comments that 
          merely narrate the syntax provide no new insight.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>It breaches the public abstraction boundary:</strong> The public Javadoc pages 
          generated by Eclipse or the <c>javadoc</c> tool represent the contract between your class 
          and outside programmers. A client needs to know what abstraction the class provides and 
          how to use it<mdash/>not the private mechanics of how it stores its data today.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      If you later refactor <c>Point</c> to store coordinates using polar coordinates (radius and 
      angle), or if you change an <c>ElectricVehicle</c> class to store battery energy in watt-hours 
      rather than kilowatt-hours, your public documentation should not have to change.
    </p>

    <p>
      To write effective Javadoc comments, it helps to consider who is reading them. Javadoc is 
      generated for an external audience: client programmers who will instantiate and use your 
      class without ever reading its internal source code. When you are writing Javadoc comments, 
      describe behaviors (e.g., <q>translates the point by dx and dy,</q> not <q>adds dx to the 
      private double field this.x</q>), and conceptual properties (e.g., <q>this point's 
      x-coordinate</q> rather than <q>the private double variable this.x</q>). Never refer to 
      private fields or private helper methods in public comments.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two standard Javadoc tags appear almost exclusively in the class-level comment: <c>@author</c> 
      and <c>@version</c>. The <c>@author</c> tag identifies the programmer (or team) responsible 
      for designing and maintaining the class, while <c>@version</c> tracks the current revision 
      status of the software (such as <c>1.0</c>, or a release date). Because authorship and 
      versioning pertain to the entire file rather than individual methods, place these tags at 
      the very bottom of the class-level comment block. Note that by default, the standard 
      <c>javadoc</c> tool omits these tags from generated HTML pages unless you specifically 
      include the <c>-author</c> and <c>-version</c> command-line flags (or check the corresponding 
      boxes in Eclipse's Export Javadoc wizard), ensuring you have control over whether internal 
      development metadata is published in the external API manual.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-9-3-constructors-methods-state">
    <title>Documenting Constructors and Method State Interactions</title>

    <p>
      Documenting constructors looks very similar to documenting methods, with two additional 
      considerations:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>Constructors have <c>@param</c> tags, but never a <c>@return</c> tag:</strong> 
          Constructors initialize newly allocated memory on the heap; in Java syntax, they have 
          no return type<mdash/>not even <c>void</c>. Therefore, including a <c>@return</c> tag 
          on a constructor will trigger a Javadoc compiler warning.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>Postconditions must specify the initialized state:</strong> A constructor's 
          postcondition should describe the initial state of the object (e.g., <q>This Point is 
          initialized at the Cartesian origin (0.0, 0.0)</q>).
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      In Chapter 4, method preconditions and postconditions only dealt with parameters and return 
      values (e.g., <q>Precondition: n &gt;= 0</q>, <q>Postcondition: returns n!</q>). With 
      classes, methods routinely inspect and modify instance state, even if they accept no arguments:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>A mutator's postcondition must explicitly document side effects on state:</strong> 
          <q>Postcondition: This point's x-coordinate is replaced with the specified value; the 
          y-coordinate is unchanged.</q>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <strong>An accessor's postcondition must guarantee that state remains unmutated:</strong> 
          <q>Postcondition: Returns this rectangle's distance from the origin; the state of this 
          rectangle is unchanged.</q>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-9-4-documenting-fields">
    <title>Documenting Fields: Constants, Counters, and Instance State</title>

    
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Just as we document classes, constructors, and methods, we also write Javadoc comments for 
        the fields declared inside a class. However, because fields can be static or non-static, 
        public or private, the purpose and audience of each field comment depends entirely on its role:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            A field Javadoc comment sits directly above the field declaration using the standard 
            <c>/** ... */</c> syntax.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Unlike methods, field comments do not use <c>@param</c> or <c>@return</c> tags. A field 
            comment consists of a concise description of what the field represents, any units of 
            measurement (such as pixels, miles, or units), and its allowable range or invariants.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        We will look at the three distinct kinds of fields you will encounter in classes like <c>Point</c>: 
        public static constants, private static tracking fields (such as a class-wide counter), and 
        private instance fields (an individual object's state).
      </p>
    </introduction>



    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-9-4-1-public-constants">
      <title>Documenting Public Static Final Fields (Constants)</title>

      <p>
        When a class defines a constant using <c>public static final</c>, that value is part of 
        the class's public interface. Anyone using your class can read the field (like <c>Math.PI</c>) 
        directly from outside the class. Therefore, public static fields must have full Javadoc comments.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your documentation for a public constant should describe what the constant represents 
        conceptually and when or why a client programmer should use it. For example, if we add 
        an <c>ORIGIN</c> constant to our <c>Point</c> class, we might declare and document it like this:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
    /**
     * An immutable Point positioned at the Cartesian origin (0.0, 0.0).
     * Useful as a default reference position or origin marker.
     */
    public static final Point ORIGIN = new Point(0.0, 0.0);
      </program>

      <p>
        When you generate Javadoc HTML pages, this comment will appear prominently under the Field 
        Summary and Field Detail sections, right alongside the constructors and methods.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-9-4-2-private-static-counters">
      <title>Documenting Private Static Fields (Counters and Shared State)</title>

      <p>
        In Section 6.5, you learned that static fields belong to the class as a whole rather than 
        to any single instance on the heap. A classic use for a static variable is a private counter 
        that tracks how many objects have been instantiated, like the <c>pointCount</c> field in the 
        <c>Point</c> class:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
    private static int pointCount = 0;
      </program>

      <p>
        Because this field is <c>private</c>, client code cannot see or modify <c>Point.pointCount</c> 
        directly. By default, the standard <c>javadoc</c> tool and Eclipse's Export Javadoc wizard 
        only generate documentation for <c>public</c> and <c>protected</c> members. Comments on 
        private fields will not appear in the client-facing HTML manual.
      </p>

      <p>
        However, writing a Javadoc comment above a private static counter is still an essential 
        software engineering habit. It serves a different, vital audience: you and the developers 
        maintaining or extending the class's source code.
      </p>

      <p>
        When documenting a private static counter, explain:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            What the counter tracks (e.g., total objects created vs. active objects).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Where and when it is modified (typically incremented inside constructors).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Any associated public accessor (such as a <c>public static getPointCount()</c> method) 
            through which clients can safely inspect the tally.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
    /**
     * The total number of Point objects instantiated during program execution.
     * Incremented in each constructor; exposed publicly via getPointCount().
     */
    private static int pointCount = 0;
      </program>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-9-4-3-private-instance-fields">
      <title>Documenting Private Instance Fields</title>

      <p>
        Finally, what about our private instance variables, like <c>private double x;</c> and 
        <c>private double y;</c>?
      </p>

      <p>
        As with private static counters, comments on private instance variables do not appear in 
        the public Javadoc manual. Their purpose is internal clarity for maintainers:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            State what piece of state the variable holds.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Document any internal constraints, expected units, or special flags (e.g., whether a 
            reference can be <c>null</c>, or if an angle is stored in radians vs. degrees).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Keep them concise and focused on internal invariant maintenance.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <xref ref="listing-point-fully-documented"/> is the complete <c>Point</c> class with the 
        class, all fields, and all methods fully documented:
      </p>

      <listing xml:id="listing-point-fully-documented">
        <caption>A fully documented <c>Point</c> class</caption>
        <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
/**
 * Represents a specific point in a two-dimensional Cartesian coordinate system.
 * Point coordinates are real numbers on an infinite plane.
 * 
 * Class Invariants:
 * - Coordinates x and y are finite real numbers.
 * 
 * @author Maggie
 * @version 1.0
 */
public class Point {

    /**
     * An immutable Point positioned at the Cartesian origin (0.0, 0.0).
     * Useful as a default reference position or origin marker.
     */
    public static final Point ORIGIN = new Point(0.0, 0.0);

    /**
     * The total number of Point objects instantiated during program execution.
     * Incremented in each constructor; inspected via getPointCount().
     */
    private static int pointCount = 0;

    /**
     * The horizontal Cartesian coordinate on the x-axis.
     */
    private double x;

    /**
     * The vertical Cartesian coordinate on the y-axis.
     */
    private double y;

    /**
     * Constructs a new Point at the specified (x, y) coordinates.
     * 
     * Precondition: x and y are finite numbers (not NaN or infinite).
     * Postcondition: This Point is initialized with the specified coordinates,
     *                and the class point counter is incremented by one.
     * 
     * @param x the initial horizontal Cartesian coordinate
     * @param y the initial vertical Cartesian coordinate
     */
    public Point(double x, double y) {
        assert !Double.isNaN(x) &amp;&amp; !Double.isInfinite(x) : "x must be a valid number.";
        assert !Double.isNaN(y) &amp;&amp; !Double.isInfinite(y) : "y must be a valid number.";
        this.x = x;
        this.y = y;
        Point.pointCount++; 
    }
    
    /**
     * Constructs a new Point with the same coordinates as an existing Point.
     * 
     * Precondition: other != null
     * 
     * @param other the Point object to copy
     */
    public Point(Point other) {
        assert other != null : "Cannot copy a null Point";
        this(other.x, other.y);
    }
    
    /**
     * Returns the total number of Point objects that have been created.
     * 
     * Precondition: None.
     * Postcondition: Returns the cumulative point instantiation count.
     * 
     * @return the total number of Point instances created
     */
    public static int getPointCount() {
        return Point.pointCount;
    }
    
    /**
     * Returns the horizontal coordinate of this Point.
     * 
     * Precondition: None.
     * Postcondition: Returns the x-coordinate; the state of this Point is unchanged.
     * 
     * @return the horizontal Cartesian coordinate
     */
    public double getX() {
        return this.x;
    }
    
    /**
     * Returns the vertical coordinate of this Point.
     * 
     * Precondition: None.
     * Postcondition: Returns the y-coordinate; the state of this Point is unchanged.
     * 
     * @return the vertical Cartesian coordinate
     */
    public double getY() {
        return this.y;
    }

    /**
     * Calculates the straight-line Euclidean distance from this point
     * to the origin (0.0, 0.0).
     * 
     * @return the distance to (0.0, 0.0)
     */
    public double distanceFromOrigin() {
        return Math.sqrt((this.x * this.x) + (this.y * this.y));
    }

    /**
     * Shifts the point's position by dx and dy.
     * 
     * @param dx the distance to move along the x-axis
     * @param dy the distance to move along the y-axis
     */
    public void translate(double dx, double dy) {
        this.x += dx;
        this.y += dy;
    }
    
    /**
     * Returns a string representation of this Point in standard (x, y) coordinate format.
     * 
     * Precondition: None.
     * Postcondition: Returns a formatted String containing this Point's coordinates;
     *                the state of this Point is unchanged.
     * 
     * @return a string formatted as "(x, y)" representing this point's coordinates
     */
    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "(" + this.x + ", " + this.y + ")";
    }

    /**
     * Indicates whether some other object is "equal to" this Point.
     * Two points are considered equal if they are the exact same instance,
     * or if the other object is also a Point with identical x and y coordinates.
     * 
     * Precondition: None (obj may be null or any object type).
     * Postcondition: Returns true if obj is an instance of Point with coordinates
     *                matching this point; false otherwise. The state of this Point
     *                and obj are unchanged.
     * 
     * @param obj the reference object with which to compare
     * @return true if this Point is equal to the obj argument; false otherwise
     */
    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        boolean equal = false;
        if (this == obj) {
            equal = true;
        } else if (obj != null &amp;&amp; obj.getClass() == this.getClass()) {
            Point other = (Point) obj;
            equal = (Double.compare(this.x, other.x) == 0 
                     &amp;&amp; Double.compare(this.y, other.y) == 0);
        }
        return equal;
    } 
}
        </program>
      </listing>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>
</section>